From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gregory Mitchell <me@gmitch215.xyz>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:20:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: read the argument tables' address from the top of the
 stack

_start took the argument tables to sit at the page-aligned address above
its stack pointer, so the kernel had to put them at the top of the
program's stack mapping, and the stack and the arguments were one
mapping of 33 pages, a 64-page block from the page allocator. The kernel
now maps the arguments apart and leaves their address in the word at the
top of the stack. _start still finds the top by rounding up the address of
a local, and reads the word below it.
---
--- a/arch/wasm/crt_arch.h
+++ b/arch/wasm/crt_arch.h
@@ -19,7 +19,9 @@

 /*
  * This must really be written in assembly (to capture the stack pointer properly), but we know that
- * the initial stack pointer is page aligned, so we can cheat a bit and align it to the next page...
+ * the initial stack pointer is just below a page aligned top of the stack, so we can cheat a bit and
+ * align it to the next page... The word at the top holds the address of the argument tables, which
+ * are in a mapping of their own.
  * The problem is that LLVM does not seem to work when functions are defined with inline asm (in C).
  * TODO: fix this hack by refactoring musl and moving things into a .S file.
  */
@@ -28,13 +30,15 @@
 {
 	void _start_c(/* no prototype on purpose */);
 	int dummy;
-	_start_c(((unsigned long)(void*)&dummy + 4095UL) & ~4095UL);
+	long *top = (long *)(((unsigned long)(void*)&dummy + 4095UL) & ~4095UL);
+	_start_c(top[-1]);
 }
 #else
 void _dlstart(void)
 {
 	void _dlstart_c(/* no prototype on purpose */);
 	int dummy;
-	_dlstart_c(((unsigned long)(void*)&dummy + 4095UL) & ~4095UL);
+	long *top = (long *)(((unsigned long)(void*)&dummy + 4095UL) & ~4095UL);
+	_dlstart_c(top[-1]);
 }
 #endif
